## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

**Summary: 10 tests, 0 meaningful assertions.** This is a textbook **coverage-touching** suite — every method is called, nothing is verified. This explains your 95% coverage with production bugs.

| Severity | Count |
|----------|-------|
| 🔴 Critical | 10 |
| 🟡 Medium | 2 |

---

### 🔴 Critical Findings

#### 1. Coverage-Touching Pattern (Systematic — entire class)

The whole test class exists to inflate coverage. All 10 tests call production methods in declaration order without verifying behavior. This is the #1 reason bugs escape.

#### 2. No Assertions (6 tests)

These tests prove nothing — they pass even if the method silently corrupts data:

| Test | Line | What's untested |
|------|------|-----------------|
| `AddProduct_Works` | 17 | Never verifies the product was stored |
| `UpdatePrice_Works` | 33 | Never verifies the price changed |
| `RemoveProduct_Works` | 39 | Never verifies the product was removed |
| `GetProductCount_Works` | 63 | Never verifies the count value |
| `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` | 69 | Never verifies prices were discounted |
| `ImportFromCsv_Works` | 85 | Never verifies the product was imported |

**Fix** (example for `AddProduct_Works`):
```csharp
// BEFORE — passes even if Save() is a no-op
var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
_catalog.AddProduct(product);

// AFTER — verifies the product was actually stored
var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
_catalog.AddProduct(product);
var retrieved = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", retrieved?.Name);
Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, retrieved?.Price);
```

**Fix** (example for `UpdatePrice_Works`):
```csharp
// BEFORE — passes even if UpdatePrice is a no-op
_catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);

// AFTER — verifies the price actually changed
_catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated?.Price);
```

**Fix** (example for `RemoveProduct_Works`):
```csharp
// BEFORE
_catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");

// AFTER
_catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
```

#### 3. Trivial `Assert.IsNotNull` Only (4 tests)

These tests assert that a reference isn't null but never check the *value*. `GetProduct` returns a non-null object even if it has the wrong SKU, name, or price — these tests would never catch that.

| Test | Line | What the assertion misses |
